# Rust often needs a bit of an extra main task stack size compared to C (the default is 3K)
#
# Raised well above the template's 8192: Rust allocates a function's whole frame on entry,
# and `main` here holds the display's pixel buffer, the UART read buffer, and the driver
# structs at once. 8192 overflowed, which surfaced as a corrupted pointer inside whichever
# ESP-IDF call happened to be deepest rather than as a stack error. Spike 3 left only ~6.5KB
# spare at 32768, and this spike adds the BLE handles on top, so it goes up again — the
# startup log reports what was actually used.
CONFIG_ESP_MAIN_TASK_STACK_SIZE=49152

# Increase a bit these stack sizes as they are also a bit too small by default
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_EVENT_TASK_STACK_SIZE=4096
CONFIG_FREERTOS_IDLE_TASK_STACKSIZE=4096

# You might have to increase this further if you spawn your own Rust threads 
# that allocate large stack variables; or better yet - use 
# `std::thread::Builder::new().stack_size(XXX)` for spawning
CONFIG_PTHREAD_TASK_STACK_SIZE_DEFAULT=4096

# PSRAM is deliberately **off** here, unlike the earlier spikes.
#
# Spike 0 enabled it on the assumption that "crux_core + serde + NimBLE want the headroom".
# That was never measured, and it is not true: main peaks around 26KB against ~126KB of free
# internal DRAM, so PSRAM buys nothing here and is one less thing in the picture.
#
# It was also suspected of causing the memory corruption this spike hit. It is not: the same
# crash occurs with PSRAM off. Kept off because it is unnecessary, not as a fix.
#
# If a later spike genuinely needs PSRAM, add it back deliberately and keep it out of the
# general heap (`heap_caps_malloc` with `MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM`) rather than via `USE_MALLOC`.
# CONFIG_SPIRAM is not set

# `esp32-nimble` needs the NimBLE host stack rather than Bluedroid, which is the default.
# Leaving Bluedroid enabled builds the wrong stack and the BLE calls find nothing.
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLE_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLUEDROID_ENABLED=n
CONFIG_BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED=y

# GATT server callbacks (`on_connect`, `on_disconnect`) run on the NimBLE **host** task, not
# on main, and its default stack is 4096 — small enough that logging from a callback can
# overflow it. That arrives as a `Double exception` with a corrupted backtrace, because the
# exception handler has no stack left to report on, and it only happens when a client
# connects or disconnects. Raising main's stack does nothing for this.
CONFIG_BT_NIMBLE_HOST_TASK_STACK_SIZE=8192

# A watchpoint on the last words of every task's stack traps an overflow at the instant it
# happens and names the task, rather than letting it corrupt a neighbour and fail later
# somewhere unrelated. Kept on: these spikes hit stack overflow twice, and both times it
# presented as a fault in innocent code — once inside an SPI driver, once as a `Double
# exception` with an unusable backtrace.
CONFIG_FREERTOS_WATCHPOINT_END_OF_STACK=y
